-- title:
--   Vanishing of continuous H² at the archimedean component
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, let $S$ be a finite set of rational primes, and let $M$ be a representation of the absolute Galois group $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ (realised as $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \simeq_{\mathbb{Q}} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ the chosen algebraic closure) on a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space. Consider the component of the family of local groups indexed by `extArithIndex S` $= \mathrm{Unit} \oplus S$ at the left-hand index `Sum.inl ()`: the associated group is the subgroup `archimedeanDecomposition` of the absolute Galois group, and the associated homomorphism `extArithLoc S (Sum.inl ())` is `archimedeanLoc`, the inclusion of that subgroup; in particular this component does not depend on $S$. Assume that the first group cohomology $H^1$ of the restriction of $M$ along this inclusion is a subsingleton, i.e. vanishes. The conclusion is that the group `continuousH2` attached to this inclusion and to the restricted module is a subsingleton, where `continuousH2` is by definition the quotient of the subgroup `levelCocycles₂` of inhomogeneous $2$-cocycles by the preimage in it of the subgroup `levelCoboundaries₂`.
--
--   This is the archimedean input to the local Euler-characteristic bookkeeping: for the decomposition group generated by complex conjugation, which is cyclic of order dividing $2$, periodicity of the cohomology of finite cyclic groups makes $H^2$ vanish as soon as $H^1$ does, and the level (continuity) conditions impose no extra restriction there. It is used in the computation of continuous $H^2$ for coinduced coefficients and in the Greenberg–Wiles style formula for the family `extArithLoc`.
-- source:
